  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Consider the following reaction:<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#916;</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>58</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></math><br />For each of the following cases <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mo>)</mo></math>, the direction in which the equilibrium shifts is:<br />(a) Temperature is decreased.<br />(b) Pressure is increased by adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> at constant T.</p>"
  },
  "options": [
